Fix an out-of-bounds read in unserialize() when a version-3 stream
labels a native string with the platform's wide-character encoding.

ReadChar() reads a length-delimited string into a buffer of exactly
length + 1 bytes (a VLA on the C stack for short strings) and, when
converting it to UTF-8 fails, hands the buffer to reEnc3() to render
the invalid-string warning.  reEnc3() decides from the encoding name
whether its input is a wide string, and for the platform's wchar_t
encoding (UCS-4LE/UCS-4BE, UTF-16LE on Windows) measures it with
wcslen(), which scans in sizeof(wchar_t) steps until it finds a wide
NUL.  A crafted stream declares that encoding and supplies a string
shorter than one wchar_t, so wcslen() runs off the end of the buffer:

    ==41067==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: dynamic-stack-buffer-overflow
    READ of size 72 at 0x00016b389382 thread T0
        #0 wcslen
        #1 reEncodeIconv         src/main/sysutils.c:2241
        #2 Rf_reEnc3             src/main/sysutils.c:2394
        #3 invalid_utf8_warning  src/main/serialize.c:1697
        #4 ReadChar              src/main/serialize.c:1753
        #5 ReadItem_Recursive    src/main/serialize.c:2028
        #9 R_Unserialize         src/main/serialize.c:2294

Add reEnc4(), which takes the input byte length explicitly, and use it
where the caller already knows that length: the serialization warning
path, the invalid-CHARSXP diagnostics in envir.c, and the buffered
wide-string conversion in sysutils.c.  reEnc3() stays as the wrapper
for NUL-terminated inputs.  Also reject an embedded NUL in a version-3
encoding name, in both unserialize() and infoRDS().

Found by fuzzing R with libFuzzer + AddressSanitizer (OSS-Fuzz
unserialize target, testcase 5161193048965120, reproducible).  A
30-byte ASCII-format stream is enough to trigger it.

Upstream status: reported on R Bugzilla bug 19047 (comment 6,
attachment 3851) with this fix attached (also r-devel/r-svn PR #339).
The upstream patch also adds a regression test to tests/reg-tests-1b.R,
left out here so this carry patch does not go stale with that file.
Delete this patch once the fix lands in R trunk (ossfuzz.sh will report
it as already-applied in the meantime, which is harmless).

